<h1>About Wavetify</h1>
<p class="updated">Last updated: November 16, 2026</p>

<p class="legal-note">
  Wavetify is a free, open-source (MIT) hobby project for listening to music, watching music
  videos and finding something good to play — no account, no ads, no subscription and no app to
  install.
</p>

<h2>What it is</h2>
<p>
  An open-source Spotify alternative: a small player that runs entirely in your browser. It
  streams full tracks from Audius and Jamendo, podcast episodes from Apple Podcasts, live radio
  from Radio Browser and music videos from YouTube. There is no backend, no database and nothing
  to sign up for.
</p>
<p>
  Wavetify is free software, released under the MIT licence. The whole source is on
  <a href="https://github.com/dnysaz/wavetify" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>,
  so anyone can read it, learn from it, fork it and run their own copy.
</p>

<h2>Where the media comes from</h2>
<p>
  Nothing is hosted, uploaded or re-hosted here. Audio and video play directly from the service
  that provides them, and every detail panel links back to the original page. That is also why
  the catalogue is whatever those free APIs offer on a given day: tracks, videos and stations can
  disappear or change without notice.
</p>
<p>
  Songs play in full from Audius and Jamendo — no 30-second previews. Podcast episodes come from
  Apple's podcast feeds. Radio is a live stream and cannot be seeked. YouTube videos play
  through YouTube's own embedded player, so they need the uploader to allow embeds.
</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>
  Wavetify is built with <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a>
  and ships as plain HTML, CSS and TypeScript. All API requests happen in your browser. Likes,
  play history, play counts, volume and your last queue are kept in this browser's
  <code>localStorage</code>; they never leave the device.
</p>
<p>
  Videos are optional for the site owner: they need a public YouTube Data API key at build time.
  Without one, the rest of the player works and the Videos page explains that it is not
  configured.
</p>

<h2>Privacy</h2>
<p>
  No accounts, no analytics and no tracking pixels. Your listening data stays in
  <code>localStorage</code> on this device. The third-party services still see the requests your
  browser makes to them, and the embedded YouTube player is provided by Google, which may set
  cookies or collect usage data under its own privacy policy.
</p>

<h2>Cost and limits</h2>
<p>
  Wavetify is free to use and free to run because it only uses public, keyless APIs — plus the
  YouTube quota, which is why video picks are cached. There is no service-level agreement and no
  guarantee that anything keeps working. It is a hobby project, not a commercial service.
</p>

<h2>Source, issues and terms</h2>
<p>
